<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qudshanis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kochanis, Konak</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qudshanis is a village in Hakkâri Province in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. From the 17th century until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it was the seat of the Shimun line of patriarchs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>, who led the Assyrian Christian tribes of the Hakkari mountains. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> were driven from the region during the massacres and wars of 1914–1918, and the patriarchate moved into exile.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The village stands in a remote part of the Hakkari mountains, long cut off from the outside world; a German traveller reached it in 1829, and Western emissaries followed. One of them, the Englishman William Ainger Wigram, described it in The Cradle of Mankind: Life in Eastern Kurdistan (1922) as lying on a sloping alp of rugged pasture between two mountain torrents, which meet below a tongue-shaped plateau and flow on to the Zab. Nestorian tradition, he noted, regarded the Zab as the Pison, one of the four rivers of Paradise.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Qudshanis was founded in 1672 by Chaldean Catholics from the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amida">Amida</a>. After settling there they broke with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> and in 1692 founded a new branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a>, ruled by the Shimun line of patriarchs. From then on the village was the de facto capital of the Assyrian tribes of the region.</p><p>The Hakkari mountains were governed as a tribal confederation. Assyrian tribes such as the Tyari and the Nochiya lived in villages across the region under their own leaders, the maliks. They paid taxes to the patriarch at Qudshanis, who passed them on to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, so that the patriarch acted as a kind of king of the mountain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> and the confederation was in effect almost a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state of the Ottoman Empire, its people owing obedience to the patriarch rather than the sultan.</p><p>When the patriarch declared war in 1915, the tribes rose in open rebellion against the Turks. In the middle of that year Mar Shimun XIX Benyamin and the rest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> of Hakkari were forced to flee during the Sayfo, ending more than two centuries as the patriarchal seat. Shimun XXI Eshai (1908–1975), patriarch of the Assyrian Church of the East, is counted among the village's notable people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Qudshanis, also written Kuçanis or Kochanes, Koçanis in Kurdish and Qūdšānīs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a>, is now officially named Konak. It is a small village in the Hakkâri District of Hakkâri Province. It is inhabited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> of the Pinyanişî tribe, and its population was 30 in 2023.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Qudshanis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The emirs ruled a remote mountain land of Kurdish tribes and semi-autonomous Assyrian Christian tribes whose patriarch lived at <b>Qudshanis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-hakkari">Emirate of Hakkari</a></li></ul></section>
